WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.860
Let's face it: nobody is ever going to call
Batman well-adjusted.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.280
He dresses up like a rodent and fights crime.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.290
He hangs out in a cave with an old man and
a rotating bunch of kids.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.180
He picks fights with Superman.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:21.780
Oh, and he's got a serious ego problem, too:
everything has to be a bat-mobile, a bat-plane,

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:23.070
or a bat-signal.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.029
Even his dog is a bat-hound—and not, say,
just a regular dog.

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:31.179
Still, there's a difference between charmingly
eccentric and outright crazy.

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:34.030
And every now and then, Batman crosses that
line.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.840
Here are some of the most terrible things
Batman's ever done.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.060
"Oh! Are you here for some..."

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.390
He left a villain to starve

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:49.329
The late-'80s villain the KGBeast has become
a fan favorite thanks to his ridiculous name

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:51.789
and delightfully dated Cold War gimmick.

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:55.940
But his first appearance ends in one of Batman's
all-time cruelest acts.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:02.120
In Batman #420, KGBeast attempts to assassinate
President Reagan, so Batman chases him through

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.590
Gotham's sewers, clobbers him with a soggy
corpse he finds there — which seriously

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:10.910
raises questions about Gotham's toilets — and
herds the Beast into an empty room.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:15.760
When KGBeast sees that he's cornered, he turns
and challenges Batman to a fight… but Batman

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.790
just closes the door and blocks it off, making
it impossible to open, leaving KGBeast to

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.170
die in a rat-infested tomb.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.670
Justifying his actions, Batman thinks, "I'm
not in this business to protect the rules.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.600
I serve justice."

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.340
It's just too bad that the rule that Batman
is breaking is the one about, y'know, not

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.400
killing people.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.200
Even later writers were horrified.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.570
To make him less of a murderous maniac, eventually
they wrote a story where Batman revealed that

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.310
he'd actually called the cops after trapping
the Beast — who managed to escape anyhow.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.850
He re-orphaned Robin

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.660
You could argue that subjecting troubled teens
to dangerous training exercises, and then

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:57.530
sending them out to fight psychopaths is terrible
all on its own — and, well, you wouldn't

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:58.530
be wrong.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.180
Yet, that's hardly the worst thing Batman
has done to his Robins.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.850
For example, in the Silver Age comic book
World's Finest #153 — billed on the cover

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.940
as an "imaginary novel," as though all the
other comic books actually happened — Bruce

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.920
Wayne becomes convinced that Superboy killed
his parents and plots his revenge against

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.200
the Last Son of Krypton.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.650
When Robin protests, Batman slaps him so viciously
that the smack traveled through time and became

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.540
an Internet meme in the future.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.070
When Robin won't relent, Batman sits him in
chair and uses a villain's hypnosis machine

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.950
to erase all of Dick Grayson's memories, before
dropping him off at an orphanage so he won't

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:34.950
get in the way.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.500
Of course, Superboy didn't kill the Waynes,
and a guilt-stricken Batman sacrifices his

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.000
life to save Superman.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.380
But nobody tells Robin, who presumably languishes
in the orphanage until he's old enough to

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.240
live on his own and become an accountant or
something.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.720
Even in regular comic continuity, Robins get
a raw deal.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.950
Batman doesn't intervene in time when Robin
number two, Jason Todd, is beaten half to

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:00.350
death with a crowbar by the Joker — and
then killed in an explosion.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:04.930
Later, Batman admits Jason was too young and
impulsive to be Robin, but that didn't stop

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.130
Bruce from slapping a mask on him anyway.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:12.030
It happened again in 2013, when an assassin
killed the fifth Robin, Damian Wayne.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:17.510
You'd think that a smart guy like Batman could
get it together...but it actually gets worse.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.340
He got Robin's girlfriend killed

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:24.630
In 2004, the third Robin, Tim Drake, hung
up his cape after his father discovered his

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:25.800
secret identity.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:31.771
In Robin #126, Stephanie Brown, Robin's long-term
girlfriend and a vigilante herself, sews her

00:03:31.771 --> 00:03:36.620
own Robin costume and sneaks into the Batcave,
hoping to gank Tim's old gig.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.400
Batman hires her on the spot: not because
Stephanie's qualified for the position or

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.310
anything, but so he can make Tim jealous.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.890
Stephanie does the best she can, but her career
as Robin ends just two issues later when Batman

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.920
fires her for disobeying orders.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.910
In order to earn back Batman's trust, Stephanie
sets one of Bruce's top-secret agendas into

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:59.120
motion, starting a gang war that's supposed
to unite all of Gotham's criminals under the

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.940
control of Matches Malone, one of Batman's
undercover aliases.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.690
It doesn't work, and violence consumes the
city.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:10.130
For her mistakes, Stephanie is captured by
the Black Mask, one of Gotham's gang leaders,

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.290
and fatally tortured.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.550
All for the love of Batman.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.930
He became a drug addict on purpose

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.659
What happens when Batman can't get the job
done?

00:04:18.659 --> 00:04:20.430
He resorts to substance abuse.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.190
In Legends of the Dark Knight #16, a young
girl drowns when Batman can't lift a boulder

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.090
that traps her in a flooded tunnel.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.740
He decides he isn't strong enough, and when
a few extra hours of Crossfit don't cut it,

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.379
he starts taking steroids to enhance his strength.

00:04:34.379 --> 00:04:39.110
The problem — other than the fact that drugs
are, y'know, usually kinda terrible — is

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:43.580
that the stuff Batman takes is the same junk
that gives Bane his super-strength.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.040
And that…. doesn't always end well.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.259
Batman juices himself enough to throw a refrigerator
through a brick wall, but he also develops

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:56.699
nasty mean streak.

00:04:56.699 --> 00:05:00.710
Once Batman is totally addicted, the scientist
providing him with Venom tells says that he'll

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.110
need to kill Commissioner Gordon if he wants
any more pills.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:08.610
Gordon lives, obviously, and Alfred locks
Batman in the Batcave for a month while he

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.909
detoxes using sheer Bat-willpower and the
power of the Bat-beard.

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:17.249
Now recovered, Batman eventually tracks down
the scientist to shut the operation down,

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:20.449
no drugs required, and arrests him fair and
square.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:22.600
He sent a guy to die in space

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.189
Lord Death Man, as the name kind of implies,
always comes back from the dead.

00:05:27.189 --> 00:05:32.550
"Batman and Robin once again triumphed over
their archenemy, Lord Death Man, after he

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.099
returned from the grave!"

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:37.560
Given that Batman doesn't usually kill, that
shouldn't be a problem.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.129
Most of the time, he just locks his villains
away in Arkham Asylum and prays they won't

00:05:41.129 --> 00:05:42.129
get out.

00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:46.270
But for some reason, Batman decides to deal
with Lord Death Man in a much more permanent

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.879
manner in Batman Incorporated #2.

00:05:48.879 --> 00:05:52.990
He seals the villain in a rocket and launches
him into space, where he'll spend the rest

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:54.639
of time completely alone.

00:05:54.639 --> 00:05:59.590
Lord Death Man's casually cruel fate is made
especially weird in the comic's final panels,

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:04.249
when Bruce Wayne and Selina Kyle calmly share
a drink while Lord Death Man's ship twinkles

00:06:04.249 --> 00:06:08.289
in the sky above, beginning its long journey
towards absolutely nowhere.

00:06:08.289 --> 00:06:13.129
Or until it runs into one of the billions
and billions of aliens that populate the DC

00:06:13.129 --> 00:06:14.129
Universe.

00:06:14.129 --> 00:06:15.129
Which it does.

00:06:15.129 --> 00:06:16.680
Nice job, Batman.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.650
He made plans to kill his teammates

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:24.020
JLA's Tower of Babel arc shows what happens
when a supervillain discovers Batman's plans

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:27.819
for beating his Justice League teammates,
and the results aren't pretty.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:31.969
After some bad guys temporarily switch bodies
with the Justice League, Batman decides to

00:06:31.969 --> 00:06:36.569
keep detailed files about every League member,
including ways to bring them down.

00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:41.069
Unfortunately, those files fall into the hands
of Ra's al Ghul, who promptly uses them in

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:42.880
a bid to take over the world.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.340
Things go bad pretty quickly.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.309
Aquaman receives a custom dose of the Scarecrow's
fear gas, making him terrified of water, which

00:06:49.309 --> 00:06:50.889
he needs to survive.

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:54.990
Hypnotic suggestion blinds Green Lantern,
robbing him of the ability to control his

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.029
power ring.

00:06:56.029 --> 00:07:00.180
Superman's skin turns translucent thanks to
some Red Kryptonite, which kicks his solar

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.399
receptors into overdrive, causing unimaginable
pain.

00:07:03.399 --> 00:07:07.169
A custom-made bullet gives Flash paralyzing
seizures.

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:10.439
Understandably, everyone gets pretty mad at
ol' Batman.

00:07:10.439 --> 00:07:14.599
They hold a vote about keeping him on the
team… and Batman just cuts out before they

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:16.339
get to make the choice.

00:07:16.339 --> 00:07:20.759
"If you people can't see the potential danger
of an out of control Justice League, I don't

00:07:20.759 --> 00:07:23.149
need to wait for a vote.

00:07:23.149 --> 00:07:24.860
I don't belong here."

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.380
He punks out

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:30.529
Batman needs Gotham City, and Gotham City
needs Batman.

00:07:30.529 --> 00:07:32.849
Just look at what happens when he's not around.

00:07:32.849 --> 00:07:37.379
In Batman Beyond, Bruce Wayne hangs up the
cape and cowl after having a heart attack

00:07:37.379 --> 00:07:41.629
mid-fight and being forced to aim a gun at
a criminal to prevent his death.

00:07:41.629 --> 00:07:45.839
Decades after Bruce Wayne's retirement from
the nightlife, Gotham City falls into the

00:07:45.839 --> 00:07:49.279
hands of a clown-themed street gang called
the Jokerz.

00:07:49.279 --> 00:07:54.550
Fortunately, Amanda Waller made a secret contingency
plan for the Caped Crusader, insuring that

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.819
a new, younger hero would inherit the mantle
of Batman.

00:07:57.819 --> 00:08:02.059
And then there's The Dark Knight Returns,
Frank Miller's infamous take on Batman's ultimate

00:08:02.059 --> 00:08:03.059
future.

00:08:03.059 --> 00:08:07.220
Batman gives up after Jason Todd's death,
and in his absence a gang called the Mutants

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.349
transform Gotham City into a war-torn dystopia.

00:08:10.349 --> 00:08:14.809
With old man Bruce brooding in Wayne Manor
for a decade, Superman becomes a pawn of the

00:08:14.809 --> 00:08:20.330
government, superherodom gets outlawed, and
— as expected — crime is on the rise everywhere.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.629
You know who could've stopped all of this
from happening?

00:08:23.629 --> 00:08:24.629
Batman.

00:08:24.629 --> 00:08:28.629
Bruce Wayne dedicated his life to keeping
Gotham City safe, and spent years protecting

00:08:28.629 --> 00:08:29.629
the city.

00:08:29.629 --> 00:08:31.969
It's hardly a surprise that Gotham came to
rely on him.

00:08:31.969 --> 00:08:36.120
It's even arguable that Batman's presence
in Gotham entices supervillains looking for

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.120
a challenge.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.110
Suddenly disappearing isn't just irresponsible
— retiring is, quite literally, the worst

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.320
thing that Batman could possibly do.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.440
Well, retiring, or doing more of this in public:

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:55.839
Thanks for watching!

00:08:55.839 --> 00:08:58.690
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.290
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

